{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Byte-identity check done 2026-10-19 on origin/main c0cf1022671 with `PYPANDOC_PANDOC=~/.local/pandoc-3.9/pandoc-3.9-arm64/bin/pandoc`:\n\n- `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler -k 'wiki or pandoc or render'`: **104 passed, 1 failed**. All 16 fixture-comparison tests in `test_wiki_render_hardening.py` pass, so the arm64 pandoc 3.9 reproduces `tests/fixtures/dc_wiki_legacy_outputs.json` byte for byte; no fixture regeneration is needed.\n- The single failure is `test_the_installed_pandoc_matches_the_legacy_fixture_provenance`: `Pandoc binary SHA-256 mismatch for darwin-arm64: expected d7b1e75cd20ee6…, installed 140353e19f25…`, raised at `scripts/generate_dc_wiki_legacy_outputs.py:150`. The expected value is the pypandoc-binary Intel payload's hash, confirmed earlier in this ticket.\n\nSo option (a) is proven safe: `SUPPORTED_PANDOC_BINARY_SHA256[\"darwin-arm64\"]` becomes a set of two accepted fingerprints (Intel `d7b1e75c…` for the default wheel under Rosetta, arm64 `140353e1…` for the documented native install), the comparison at `:150` becomes membership, and the error message lists the accepted set. AC1 must be rewritten from \"pin only the arm64 fingerprint\" to \"accept both\", since the pinned-only form would break every default install. On this host the 17 `Bad CPU type` failures seen by every lane are gone once `PYPANDOC_PANDOC` is exported; `docs/local-dev-env.md` already prescribes that."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1792429494638044606,"uuid":"3916529f-7a2c-45e2-b7cb-cf0856a0cf9c"}